{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses separated by spaces.\n    For each of the group, output the deepest level of nesting of parentheses.\n    E.g. (()()) has maximum two levels of nesting while ((())) has three.\n\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('(()()) ((())) () ((())()())')\n    [2, 3, 1, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    class ParenthesesParser:\n        \"\"\"A utility class to calculate the maximum nesting depth of a single parentheses group.\"\"\"\n        def calculate_depth(self, group_string: str) -> int:\n            max_depth = 0\n            current_depth = 0\n            for char in group_string:\n                if char == '(':\n                    current_depth += 1\n                    max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n                elif char == ')':\n                    current_depth -= 1\n                    current_depth = max(0, current_depth) # Ensure depth doesn't go negative\n            return max_depth\n\n    if not paren_string.strip():\n        return []\n\n    parser = ParenthesesParser()\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n    results = []\n    for group in groups:\n        if group: # Only process non-empty groups\n            results.append(parser.calculate_depth(group))\n    return results"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef sum_product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of integers, return a tuple consisting of a sum and a product of all the integers in a list.\n    Empty sum should be equal to 0 and empty product should be equal to 1.\n    >>> sum_product([])\n    (0, 1)\n    >>> sum_product([1, 2, 3, 4])\n    (10, 24)\n    \"\"\"\n    current_sum = 0\n    current_product = 1\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_sum += number\n        current_product *= number\n\n    return (current_sum, current_product)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n    >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    rolling_max_list = []\n    current_max = numbers[0]\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num > current_max:\n            current_max = num\n        rolling_max_list.append(current_max)\n\n    return rolling_max_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/12", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\n\ndef longest(strings: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Out of list of strings, return the longest one.\n    Return the first one in case of multiple strings of the same length. Return None in case the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strings:\n        return None\n\n    longest_found = None\n    max_length = -1\n\n    for s in strings:\n        if len(s) > max_length:\n            max_length = len(s)\n            longest_found = s\n    return longest_found\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "```python\ndef string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input are two strings a and b consisting only of 1s and 0s.\n    Perform binary XOR on these inputs and return result also as a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not all(c in '01' for c in a):\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid character in string '{a}'. Only '0' and '1' are allowed.\")\n    if not all(c in '01' for c in b):\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid character in string '{b}'. Only '0' and '1' are allowed.\")\n\n    if len(a) != len(b):\n        raise ValueError(f\"Input strings must have the same length. Got '{a}' (length {len(a)}) and '{b}' (length {len(b)}).\")\n\n    result_list = []\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        xor_result = int(a[i]) ^ int(b[i])\n        result_list.append(str(xor_result))\n\n    return \"\".join(result_list)\n```"}
