{"task_id": "HumanEval/135", "solution": "```python\ndef can_arrange(arr):\n    \"\"\"Returns the largest index of an element which is not greater than or\n    equal to the element immediately preceding it. If no such element\n    exists then return -1.\"\"\"\n    if not arr or len(arr) < 2:\n        return -1\n\n    last_disruption_index = -1\n    for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n        if arr[i] < arr[i-1]:\n            last_disruption_index = i\n    return last_disruption_index\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "```python\ndef minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid with N rows and N columns (N >= 2) and a positive integer k,\n    each cell of the grid contains a value. Every integer in the range [1, N * N]\n    inclusive appears exactly once on the cells of the grid.\n\n    You have to find the minimum path of length k in the grid. You can start\n    from any cell, and in each step you can move to any of the neighbor cells,\n    in other words, you can go to cells which share an edge with you current\n    cell.\n    Please note that a path of length k means visiting exactly k cells (not\n    necessarily distinct).\n    You CANNOT go off the grid.\n    A path A (of length k) is considered less than a path B (of length k) if\n    after making the ordered lists of the values on the cells that A and B go\n    through (let's call them lst_A and lst_B), lst_A is lexicographically less\n    than lst_B, in other words, there exist an integer index i (1 <= i <= k)\n    such that lst_A[i] < lst_B[i] and for any j (1 <= j < i) we have\n    lst_A[j] = lst_B[j].\n    It is guaranteed that the answer is unique.\n    Return an ordered list of the values on the cells that the minimum path go through.\n\n    Examples:\n\n        Input: grid = [ [1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]], k = 3\n        Output: [1, 2, 1]\n\n        Input: grid = [ [5,9,3], [4,1,6], [7,8,2]], k = 1\n        Output: [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    N = len(grid)\n    min_path_global = [float('inf')] * k\n\n    def _get_neighbors(r, c):\n        \"\"\"Returns a list of valid adjacent cell coordinates (row, column) within the grid.\"\"\"\n        neighbors = []\n        directions = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)] # Right, Left, Down, Up\n\n        for dr, dc in directions:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 <= nr < N and 0 <= nc < N:\n                neighbors.append((nr, nc))\n        return neighbors\n\n    def _dfs(r, c, current_path_list):\n        \"\"\"Recursively explores paths of length k, updating min_path_global with the lexicographically smallest path.\"\"\"\n        nonlocal min_path_global\n\n        current_path_list.append(grid[r][c])\n\n        if len(current_path_list) == k:\n            if current_path_list < min_path_global:\n                min_path_global[:] = current_path_list[:]\n            current_path_list.pop()\n            return\n\n        # Pruning: If the current path prefix is already lexicographically greater than\n        # the known best path's corresponding prefix, stop exploring this branch.\n        if current_path_list >= min_path_global[:len(current_path_list)]:\n            current_path_list.pop()\n            return\n\n        for nr, nc in _get_neighbors(r, c):\n            _dfs(nr, nc, current_path_list)\n\n        current_path_list.pop() # Backtrack\n\n    for r_start in range(N):\n        for c_start in range(N):\n            _dfs(r_start, c_start, [])\n\n    return min_path_global\n```"}
